On 2007-08-11, 3cnfsat@gmail.com <3cnfsat@gmail.com> wrote:
> Two of my chaotic Sporkhack players have been "blasted by
> Stormbringer's power!" after they were granted the sword as a
> *sacrifice gift*. What's more, when my priest found it on his deity's
> barbecue pit, his broadsword skill slot was left restricted. Is this
> intentional for priests? And is the blasting behavior intentional?

The blasting behavior means you're not chaotic enough, for whatever
reason; it's in vanilla as well.  There was an issue reported by someone
else earlier where an angry god still granted a sacrifice gift; I'll
take a look and see if something went weird somewhere.

Also, the priest/monk/wizard restrictions on "never becoming
unrestricted" do apply to sacrifice gifts.  The jury (ie. me) is
currently still out on whether or not those roles should even receive
those gifts in the first place; everyone seems a bit freaked out by it,
but honestly, I'm still not sure why giving Stormbringer to, say, a pet
that wields weapons is such a bad plan.  Or, perhaps, a wizard choosing
to wield Dragonbane after having been granted it even though he knows
his to-hit is capped at 75%.

One thing I'm absolutely not in favor of is sacrifice always being a
guaranteed "major good thing" -- ie. you'll receive something that's
useful.  In vanilla, the chance of getting Ogresmasher balances out the
chance of getting one of the "good" ones; in Spork, I enhanced all the
artifacts so that none particularly suck.  If I further restrict certain
classes to only receiving "useful" items such as spellbooks or artifacts
that cater to their specialties, then I make sacrificing too good; so
I'm not going down those roads.  It really needs to be a crapshoot.

At the moment, I don't think receiving Stormbringer is ever all _that_
bad, given that it provides level drain protection and there's one
near-guaranteed source of level-drain attack for *every* game.

